WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.630 --> 00:00:02.310
So I just got home from the Moth

00:00:02.430 --> 00:00:04.170
performing at the Moth last night.

00:00:04.770 --> 00:00:08.130
It was my wow, my a hundred and

00:00:10.350 --> 00:00:12.630
sixty ninth performance at The Moth.

00:00:12.630 --> 00:00:17.640
I think about 131 Story Slams and 39 Grand

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:19.165
Slams, and actually some main stage shows.

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:20.850
A whole bunch is the point.

00:00:21.570 --> 00:00:23.370
And yet, despite all of

00:00:23.370 --> 00:00:25.050
those performances, I learned

00:00:25.050 --> 00:00:26.160
something important last night.

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:29.369
On the way to the moth, I put my

00:00:29.369 --> 00:00:30.599
story together in the car, which

00:00:30.599 --> 00:00:31.770
is not something you should really

00:00:31.770 --> 00:00:33.900
do, but I will occasionally do.

00:00:34.290 --> 00:00:36.239
I knew what my story was like I sort

00:00:36.239 --> 00:00:37.680
of know what happened, and I knew

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:39.030
what my beginning and ending were, but

00:00:39.030 --> 00:00:41.100
I didn't really have any structure.

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:42.269
I hadn't spoken it out loud.

00:00:42.269 --> 00:00:43.349
I hadn't put words to it.

00:00:43.410 --> 00:00:44.250
So I got in the car.

00:00:44.250 --> 00:00:45.720
I've got a two hour drive to Boston,

00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:46.800
and I've got two friends with me.

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:48.480
Both of them attend moths all

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:49.650
the time with me, and so they're,

00:00:49.890 --> 00:00:51.090
they're good at giving me advice.

00:00:51.794 --> 00:00:53.025
And so I sort of picked through the

00:00:53.025 --> 00:00:55.425
story once and figured out what it

00:00:55.425 --> 00:00:57.434
would be, and then I said it a couple

00:00:57.434 --> 00:00:59.745
more times, uh, you know, honing

00:00:59.745 --> 00:01:01.635
some of the moments, finding the

00:01:01.635 --> 00:01:03.974
right sentences, all of those things.

00:01:04.515 --> 00:01:05.715
And then I got to the moth and

00:01:05.715 --> 00:01:07.215
uh, my name was chosen fourth.

00:01:07.215 --> 00:01:09.615
So I told my story and I. During

00:01:09.615 --> 00:01:12.045
intermission, another storyteller who

00:01:12.045 --> 00:01:13.695
happened to be attending who knows me

00:01:13.695 --> 00:01:15.525
and know who knows that sometimes I

00:01:15.525 --> 00:01:17.295
will put a story together in the car

00:01:17.295 --> 00:01:18.645
and then perform it on stage, which

00:01:18.645 --> 00:01:20.295
again, is not the smartest thing to

00:01:20.295 --> 00:01:22.275
do, but something I occasionally do.

00:01:22.875 --> 00:01:23.985
This storyteller asked my

00:01:23.985 --> 00:01:26.145
friend Kaya, so I wanna know.

00:01:26.445 --> 00:01:28.125
Is the story that Matt told you in

00:01:28.125 --> 00:01:30.075
the car on the way to the Moth, the

00:01:30.075 --> 00:01:31.604
same story that he just told on the

00:01:31.604 --> 00:01:34.425
stage and Kaia said, absolutely not.

00:01:35.024 --> 00:01:37.155
And I was surprised by her answer,

00:01:37.155 --> 00:01:38.384
but it only took me a second to

00:01:38.384 --> 00:01:39.884
realize she was totally correct.

00:01:40.455 --> 00:01:42.285
The story I told in the car was

00:01:42.285 --> 00:01:43.395
completely different than what

00:01:43.395 --> 00:01:45.345
I had done on stage in the car.

00:01:45.345 --> 00:01:46.845
What I did was create a structure.

00:01:47.384 --> 00:01:48.525
I figured out what the scenes

00:01:48.525 --> 00:01:50.115
would be, I knew what the

00:01:50.115 --> 00:01:51.735
goals of each scene would be.

00:01:51.735 --> 00:01:53.115
I sort of developed some

00:01:53.115 --> 00:01:54.945
transitions from scene to scene.

00:01:55.935 --> 00:01:57.855
But that was about all I did in the car.

00:01:57.860 --> 00:01:59.565
I, I gave it words the first time,

00:01:59.565 --> 00:02:00.735
but the words that I gave it in

00:02:00.735 --> 00:02:01.995
the car were actually not the

00:02:01.995 --> 00:02:03.615
same words I performed on stage.

00:02:03.615 --> 00:02:06.825
I found new jokes to tell along the way.

00:02:07.245 --> 00:02:08.895
I dropped information that

00:02:08.895 --> 00:02:10.365
I felt wasn't necessary.

00:02:10.365 --> 00:02:11.895
I responded to the audience when

00:02:11.895 --> 00:02:13.725
they seemed to like something.

00:02:13.725 --> 00:02:15.255
I added a little bit to it, and

00:02:15.255 --> 00:02:16.965
when they seem not to respond

00:02:16.965 --> 00:02:18.255
to something, I pulled back.

00:02:18.945 --> 00:02:20.415
Kaya was completely right.

00:02:20.895 --> 00:02:22.485
The story on stage really

00:02:22.485 --> 00:02:23.535
came to life in a way.

00:02:23.535 --> 00:02:25.155
It had not come to life in the car.

00:02:25.155 --> 00:02:26.625
But what I did and what I tell

00:02:26.625 --> 00:02:28.665
people all the time is I gave it

00:02:28.665 --> 00:02:30.525
structure in which I could work.

00:02:30.795 --> 00:02:32.085
I don't memorize stories.

00:02:32.085 --> 00:02:33.045
I remember them.

00:02:33.315 --> 00:02:34.875
I remember what happened to me, and

00:02:34.875 --> 00:02:36.735
I create the structure so that when

00:02:36.735 --> 00:02:39.795
I'm on stage, I can be flexible and I

00:02:39.795 --> 00:02:42.585
can adjust, and I can meet the needs

00:02:42.585 --> 00:02:44.085
of the audience, and I can meet the

00:02:44.085 --> 00:02:45.945
time limit and all of those things.

00:02:46.305 --> 00:02:47.084
So structure.

00:02:47.084 --> 00:02:48.015
It's everything.

00:02:48.075 --> 00:02:48.614
It is.

00:02:49.215 --> 00:02:51.945
It is really the framework in which we

00:02:52.035 --> 00:02:54.465
operate as storytellers and performers.

00:02:54.465 --> 00:02:57.045
We need to know what the goal of

00:02:57.045 --> 00:02:59.234
each scene is, how we get from one to

00:02:59.234 --> 00:03:00.945
the other, what's the chronological

00:03:00.945 --> 00:03:02.565
structure that we've created.

00:03:02.565 --> 00:03:03.945
But ultimately, once we have that

00:03:03.945 --> 00:03:06.015
in place, we have the freedom.

00:03:06.420 --> 00:03:09.480
To adjust and create and

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:11.460
respond in meaningful ways.

00:03:11.790 --> 00:03:13.350
I won the slam that night.

00:03:13.350 --> 00:03:19.290
It was my 61st moth story slam win of

00:03:19.290 --> 00:03:24.150
about 131 story slams, and so I did well.

00:03:24.510 --> 00:03:26.490
I did well with structure.

00:03:27.105 --> 00:03:30.015
Not with memorization, not with the

00:03:30.015 --> 00:03:32.325
careful choice of vocabulary, not

00:03:32.325 --> 00:03:34.605
with sentence structure, but with

00:03:34.605 --> 00:03:36.555
an understanding about what my story

00:03:36.555 --> 00:03:38.984
was, a framework in which I could

00:03:38.984 --> 00:03:41.535
operate, and then the freedom to

00:03:41.535 --> 00:03:45.825
create, adjust, modify, be myself

00:03:45.825 --> 00:03:48.495
so that I wasn't someone who was.

00:03:49.049 --> 00:03:51.959
Reciting memorized words on stage,

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:54.959
but actually telling a story

00:03:55.230 --> 00:03:57.989
from my heart and my mind with

00:03:57.989 --> 00:03:59.459
a little bit of planning ahead.

00:03:59.880 --> 00:04:02.220
It's not the way I would advise anyone

00:04:02.220 --> 00:04:04.350
to go and perform in front of 500 people.

00:04:04.739 --> 00:04:06.209
It is not the way that you

00:04:06.209 --> 00:04:07.890
should ever think about doing it.

00:04:08.185 --> 00:04:10.260
This is one of those do, as I say,

00:04:10.260 --> 00:04:11.970
not as I do situations, but what you

00:04:11.970 --> 00:04:14.700
should be thinking is structure first.

00:04:15.165 --> 00:04:17.565
Once you understand the beginning and the

00:04:17.565 --> 00:04:19.725
end, and the scenes and the transitions

00:04:19.845 --> 00:04:21.464
and the goals of each one of those

00:04:21.464 --> 00:04:23.775
scenes, once you have that in place, the

00:04:23.775 --> 00:04:27.525
sentences that you apply become your own.

00:04:27.555 --> 00:04:29.235
And when they really are your own and

00:04:29.235 --> 00:04:31.485
not so pre-planned that they feel like

00:04:31.485 --> 00:04:34.455
they're memorized and performed, that

00:04:34.455 --> 00:04:36.645
is when storytelling becomes alive.

00:04:36.945 --> 00:04:37.125
So.

00:04:37.125 --> 00:04:38.460
Kaia was right.

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:40.800
The story I told in the car was very

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:43.560
different than the story I told on stage

00:04:43.560 --> 00:04:46.740
and yet ultimately it was the same story.

00:04:47.190 --> 00:04:49.050
So structure, structure

00:04:49.050 --> 00:04:50.370
first, everything else.

00:04:50.430 --> 00:04:51.000
Second.

